2. Accurate Inventory Custody and Quantity Management

This is one of the most important parts of the application.

The inventory system must distinguish between physical stock owned by the merchant and stock physically available for sale.

A product being outside the warehouse must not remain available just because it has not yet been delivered to the customer.

2.1 Inventory quantity categories

Use clear and mutually exclusive quantity categories.

At minimum:

- Available Stock: Physically present, inspected, sellable, and available for new orders.
- Reserved Stock: Assigned to active orders but not yet handed over to the shipping company.
- Outbound In Transit: Physically handed to a delivery company and currently outside the merchant's custody.
- Return In Transit: Returning from the customer or delivery company but not yet received.
- Awaiting Inspection: Physically received but not approved for sale.
- Damaged / Unsellable: Physically present but unavailable for sale.
- Other Confirmed Stock Adjustments: Clearly documented adjustments that do not belong to the above categories.

Show the quantities separately.

Do not add these categories together and label the result Available Stock.

2.2 Inventory movement rules

Create a reliable inventory movement engine.

Example:

Initial inventory:

- Available: 20
- Reserved: 0
- Outbound In Transit: 0
- Return In Transit: 0
- Awaiting Inspection: 0
- Damaged: 0

A customer orders 2 units:

- Available: 18
- Reserved: 2

The merchant hands the order to the shipping company:

- Available: 18
- Reserved: 0
- Outbound In Transit: 2

The customer successfully receives the order:

- Outbound In Transit: 0
- Available: 18
- Final sold quantity: 2

The customer refuses delivery and the shipment starts returning:

- Outbound In Transit: 0
- Return In Transit: 2
- Available remains 18

The return arrives at the warehouse:

- Return In Transit: 0
- Awaiting Inspection: 2
- Available remains 18

The merchant inspects and approves both units:

- Awaiting Inspection: 0
- Available: 20

If one unit is damaged:

- Awaiting Inspection: 0
- Available: 19
- Damaged: 1

These transitions must happen exactly once.

The application must never:

- Count an in-transit product as available.
- Deduct the same order quantity twice.
- Add a returned product before physical receipt and inspection.
- Increase available stock because a merchant simply changes an order status.
- Treat an unexplained quantity reduction as a confirmed sale.
- Create duplicate inventory movements when importing the same order again.

2.3 Inventory movement ledger

Every inventory movement must be recorded with:

- Product ID.
- SKU.
- Quantity.
- Movement type.
- Previous quantity.
- New quantity.
- Previous inventory state.
- New inventory state.
- Related order ID.
- Related return ID, if applicable.
- Marketplace.
- Shipping company, if applicable.
- Timestamp.
- Source of the movement.
- User who confirmed it.
- Reason or notes.

The inventory ledger must be auditable and must not silently rewrite historical movements.

Corrections should create documented adjustment movements rather than deleting history.

2.4 Inventory dashboard

The dashboard must show where inventory actually is.

Display separate quantities for:

- Available in warehouse.
- Reserved for orders.
- With delivery companies.
- Returning to warehouse.
- Awaiting inspection.
- Damaged or unsellable.

Make the distinction between merchant-owned inventory and available-to-sell inventory visually clear.

The merchant should be able to click any quantity and open the exact products and orders responsible for it.

